{
 file:"/tmp/tally-issue-neatlogs-history-pagination.md",
 title:"[Neatlogs] Paginate price-history reads within the API limit",
 body:`## Problem

Tally's remote price-history adapter requests \`limit=100\` by default (\`adapters/neatlogs-history.ts\`). During the 2026-10-11 sponsor E2E run, Neatlogs rejected that request with HTTP 400 because trace-list requests support at most 50. \`lookup()\` catches the error and silently falls back to local history, so a configured Neatlogs integration can look healthy while routing never uses remote history.

Neatlogs documents trace-list limits of 1–50 and cursor pagination: https://docs.neatlogs.com/api-reference/pagination

## Expected

- Request a supported page size and follow Neatlogs' opaque cursor while applying a clear upper bound.
- Keep the local fallback, but make it observable when remote history could not be read; don't conflate a failed remote read with “no matching observations.”
- Add coverage for the server rejecting an oversized page, multi-page aggregation, and fallback status.

## Evidence

With a 50-row request, the same adapter successfully read remote history in the E2E environment. With the default 100-row request, it fell back to the local history file.`
},
{
 file:"/tmp/tally-issue-plan-history-adapter.md",
 title:"Make `tally run --plan` use the configured price-history source",
 body:`## Problem

\`engine/plan.ts\` constructs a fresh in-memory \`PriceHistory\` for routing. It does not load the persistent local history or the configured Neatlogs history adapter used by real runs. As a result, a plan can say there is no prior history and choose a different tier/model than the next live run.

In the 2026-10-11 sponsor E2E, a live route used one previous medium/cheap attempt from local fallback history, while the plan path had no history.

## Expected

- Share the configured history-port construction between planning and real execution.
- Keep planning read-only; it should not record outcomes or mutate history.
- Show which history source was used (remote Neatlogs, local fallback, or none), and cover parity between plan and execution routing with persisted history.`
},
{
 file:"/tmp/tally-issue-model-preflight.md",
 title:"Preflight configured models against the active worker account",
 body:`## Problem

A configured model can be unavailable to the active Codex login even when \`tally doctor\` reports the worker as ready. In the 2026-10-11 sponsor E2E, the first top-tier attempt was rejected for the configured model (\`gpt-5.6-sol\`) before a model call or any spend. The issue was only resolved after checking the account's available models and remapping the scratch test config.

## Expected

- Before starting an issue, verify that each configured tier model is accepted by its selected harness/account where a non-spending capability check is available.
- If this cannot be checked reliably, report the limitation clearly and surface the worker's model rejection as a preflight/configuration error with the failing tier and a remediation hint.
- Add a regression test showing that an unavailable configured model is caught before a worker is started.`
},
